WEBVTT
Kind: subtitles
Language: en

1
00:00.360 --> 00:05.200
So when we think about where to focus our problem-solving efforts,
one of the techniques we like to use is something called the 80/20 approach.

2
00:05.200 --> 00:09.190
It's sometimes called the Pareto principle.

3
00:09.610 --> 00:15.400
And the idea behind this is that you usually get 80% of the benefits or insights
by doing 20% of the work.

4
00:15.400 --> 00:24.190
So when you think about applying the 80/20, it applies both in short-term
problem-solving time horizons, as well as long-term time horizons.

5
00:24.450 --> 00:29.400
In the short term, it's important to do because there's a very finite time
you have to solve a problem.

6
00:29.400 --> 00:38.750
And so you want to think about: what are those 20% of the analyses you may reasonably be able
to get done — let's say in one week — in order to get the best answer you can in that time?

7
00:38.890 --> 00:45.400
In a longer time horizon — over a series of weeks, over a series of months —
it's still important to think about applying the 80/20 principle,

8
00:45.400 --> 00:50.200
so not boiling the ocean — but in this case, it's particularly important
to iterate on that prioritisation approach.

9
00:50.200 --> 00:54.460
And so as you're going through the analyses,

10
00:54.690 --> 01:00.400
as new insights are being developed, you want to think about
how that might change how I'm prioritising my time.

11
01:00.400 --> 01:08.670
So for example, if one of the analyses you conduct surprises you and eliminates
one of the branches from your tree, because you've disproved that hypothesis —

12
01:08.870 --> 01:14.400
well, now you know you no longer need to do a series of analyses
that may have followed from there,

13
01:14.400 --> 01:17.400
and you can prioritise your problem-solving time appropriately.

14
01:17.400 --> 01:20.230
So what does applying the 80/20 principle look like in practice?
Let me give you an example.

15
01:20.840 --> 01:26.470
So I was once valuing a business,
and this business had well over 200 different lines of business.

16
01:26.820 --> 01:30.150
And what we needed to do was figure out, in the amount of time that we had,

17
01:30.660 --> 01:37.220
how to come up with an appropriate valuation. Now, if we'd tried to go
and value each business one by one, through all 200,

18
01:37.540 --> 01:41.400
we never would have had enough time to get to the right answer.

19
01:41.400 --> 01:46.400
And so what we needed to think about was: how do we apply the 80/20 principle?
And in this case, there were about 30 businesses

20
01:46.400 --> 01:52.570
which were going to have a disproportionate impact on the valuation. So we really focused
on valuing those businesses, to get us to the solution we needed

21
01:52.840 --> 01:55.400
to address the problem we were trying to solve.

22
01:55.400 --> 02:01.400
And that's an example where 80/20 is a guidance. It doesn't literally mean
you only get to do 20% of the analysis,

23
02:01.400 --> 02:07.400
but it's meant to prompt you to think about what the things are that you can and should
look at, that are going to get you to the answer most quickly.

24
02:07.400 --> 02:12.830
So when you're applying the 80/20 principle in the real world,
there are a couple of things that are important to keep in mind.

25
02:13.320 --> 02:16.230
One is being comfortable with ambiguity and uncertainty.

26
02:16.710 --> 02:22.630
You're getting, like, 80% of the way there to the answer — not 100%.
And at some point you need to be comfortable with that.

27
02:22.950 --> 02:29.200
And the key is to ask yourself: do you feel, directionally, you've gotten
a sufficient level of detail to move forward?

28
02:29.200 --> 02:36.100
And this can vary by context. So if you're thinking about sizing a new market
and you're trying to understand: is it a

29
02:36.550 --> 02:42.400
10-million-dollar opportunity or a 100-million opportunity —
there's probably a lower level of precision you need to get to.

30
02:42.400 --> 02:50.940
But if you're working at a semiconductor manufacturing plant and you look at the error rates,
you might actually need two or three or more decimal places of precision.

31
02:51.080 --> 02:57.860
And so think about: at what point are you comfortable enough to move on?
Because explicitly, you're not going to be able to get 100% of the way there.
